Here's an example C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string filename, word;
    int count = 0;

    cout << "Enter the filename: ";
    cin >> filename;

    ifstream file(filename);

    if (file.is_open()) {
        while (file >> word) {
            if (word == "the") {
                count++;
            }
        }
        file.close();
    } else {
        cout << "Unable to open file";
    }

    cout << "The word 'the' appears " << count << " times in the file." << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first prompt the user to enter the filename of the text file to be read. We then open the file using the `ifstream` class and read it line by line using the `>>` operator. We check each word in the file to see if it matches the target word ("the" in this example), and increment the `count` variable if it does. Finally, we print out the total number of occurrences of the target word in the file.
